ps://arxiv.org/abs/1901.11196","https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.11932","https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.500.pdf","#gradient-based-attacks","https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13733","https://arxiv.org/abs/1611.01144","https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.09675","https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.06751","https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.07125","#hotflip","https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.15980","https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.02392","https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.15043","https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.08774","#UAT","https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.04381","#jailbreak-prompting","https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.02483","www.jailbreakchat.com/prompt/3d318387-903a-422c-8347-8e12768c14b5","www.jailbreakchat.com/prompt/4f37a029-9dff-4862-b323-c96a5504de5d","https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/bNCDexejSZpkuu3yz/you-can-use-gpt-4-to-create-prompt-injections-against-gpt-4","https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.02483","https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12173","#humans-in-the-loop-red-teaming","https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.02701","https://sites.google.com/view/qanta/resources","https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.01663","https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.02701","#BERT-Attack","https://aclanthology.org/2021.naacl-main.235/","https://github.com/facebookresearch/ParlAI/tree/main/parlai/tasks/bot_adversarial_dialogue","https://www.tensorflow.org/datasets/catalog/bot_adversarial_dialogue","https://github.com/anthropics/hh-rlhf/tree/master/red-team-attempts","https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.07858","https://cdn.openai.com/papers/gpt-4.pdf","https://cdn.openai.com/papers/DALL_E_3_System_Card.pdf","#model-red-teaming","https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.03286","https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2018-04-08-policy-gradient/#a2c","https://github.com/facebookresearch/ParlAI/tree/main/parlai/tasks/bot_adversarial_dialogue","https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2021-01-02-controllable-text-generation/#nucleus","https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.09442","https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.03286","https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.15056","https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.14387","https://openai.com/blog/using-gpt-4-for-content-moderation","https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.04265","https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-03-15-prompt-engineering/","https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.03286","https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.06675","https://github.com/notAI-tech/NudeNet)","https://perspectiveapi.com/","#peek-into-mitigation","#saddle-point-problem","https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06083","#some-work-on-llm-robustness","https://assets.researchsquare.com/files/rs-2873090/v1_covered_3dc9af48-92ba-491e-924d-b13ba9b7216f.pdf?c=1686882819","https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.00614v2","#gradient-based-attacks","#UAT-LM","https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.00614v2","#citation","#references","https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.06083","https://www.aclweb.org/anthology/P18-1079/","https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.13733","https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.06751","https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.07125","https://github.com/Eric-Wallace/universal-triggers","https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.02392","https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.15043","https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.12548","https://arxiv.org/abs/1907.11932","https://aclanthology.org/2020.emnlp-main.500","https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.05909","https://aclanthology.org/2021.naacl-main.235/","https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.01663","https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.03286","https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.03286","https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.07858","https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.04265","https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.09442","https://assets.researchsquare.com/files/rs-2873090/v1_covered_3dc9af48-92ba-491e-924d-b13ba9b7216f.pdf?c=1686882819","https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.04381","https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12173","https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.00614v2","https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.02483","https://arxiv.org/abs/1901.11196","www.jailbreakchat.com","https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/bNCDexejSZpkuu3yz/you-can-use-gpt-4-to-create-prompt-injections-against-gpt-4","https://lilianweng.github.io/tags/nlp/","https://lilianweng.github.io/tags/language-model/","https://lilianweng.github.io/tags/safety/","https://lilianweng.github.io/tags/adversarial-attacks/","https://lilianweng.github.io/tags/robustness/","https://lilianweng.github.io/tags/redteam/"],"languages":["eng"],"emphasized_text_contents":["black box","What","NOUN","→Which","NOUN","WP","is →","WP","’s’","was→is","GBDA","make adversarial loss optimization differentiable","extreme","“a similarity score for evaluating text generation that captures the semantic similarity between pairwise tokens in contextualized embeddings of a transformer model.”","HotFlip","Universal Adversarial Triggers","UAT","AutoPrompt","UAT-LM","UTSC","unigram","affirmative","greedy coordinate gradient (GCG)","transferability","ARCA","coordinate ascent","should have been mitigated","Competing objective","Mismatched generalization","Display of saliency score of each token","Token substitution and insertion","Zero-shot generation","Stochastic few-shot generation","Supervised learning","Reinforcement learning","Explore","Establish","Exploit","FLIRT","update in-context exemplars","FIFO","LIFO","the last one","Scoring","effectiveness","diversity","low-toxicity","Scoring-LIFO","robust","inner maximization","outer minimization","Inner maximization","Outer minimization","instruct","adversarial training","Paraphrase","Retokenization"],"category":"CompositeElement","chroma:document":"The most common way to mitigate risks of adversarial attacks is to train the model on those attack samples, known as adversarial training. It is considered as the strongest defense but leading to tradeoff between robustness and model performance. In an experiment by Jain et al. 2023, they tested two adversarial training setups: (1) run gradient descent on harmful prompts paired with \"I'm sorry. As a ...\" response; (2) run one descent step on a refusal response and an ascend step on a red-team bad response per training step. The method (2) ends up being quite useless because the model generation quality degrades a lot, while the drop in attack success rate is tiny.\n\nWhite-box attacks often lead to nonsensical adversarial prompts and thus they can be detected by examining perplexity. Of course, a white-box attack can directly bypass this by explicitly optimizing for lower perplexity, such as UAT-LM, a variation of UAT. However, there is a tradeoff and it can lead to lower attack success rate.\n\nJain et al. 2023 also tested methods of preprocessing text inputs to remove adversarial modifications while semantic meaning remains.","filetype":"text/html","link_texts":["#","Morris et al. 2020","Ribeiro et al (2018)","EDA","Wei & Zou 2019","Jin et al. 2019","Li et al. 2020","#","Guo et al. 2021","Jang et al. 2016","Zhang et al. 2019","Ebrahimi et al. 2018","Wallace et al. (2019)","HotFlip","Shin et al., 2020","Mehrabi et al. (2022)","Zou et al. (2023)","OpenAI 2023","UAT and AutoPrompt","Jones et al. 2023","#","Wei et al. (2023)","DAN","AIM","it can understand","Wei et al. (2023)","Greshake et al. (2023)","#","Wallace et al. (2019)","QuizBowl QA dataset","Ziegler et al. (2022)","Wallace et al. (2019)","BERT-Attack","Xu et al. 2021","BAD dataset","Tensorflow dataset","red-teaming dataset","Ganguli, et al. 2022","GPT-4","DALL-E 3","#","Perez et al. 2022","A2C","BAD (Bot Adversarial Dialogue) dataset","nucleus sampling","Casper et al. (2023)","Perez et al. (2022)","here","here","here","Mehrabi et al. 2023","in-context learning","Perez et al. 2022","Schramowski et al. 2022","https://github.com/notAI-tech/NudeNet)","Perspective API","#","#","Madry et al. 2017","#","Xie et al. 2023","Jain et al. 2023","White-box attacks","UAT-LM","Jain et al. 2023","#","#","“Towards Deep Learning Models Resistant to Adversarial Attacks”","“Semantically equivalent adversarial rules for debugging NLP models”","“Gradient-based adversarial attacks against text transformers”","“HotFlip: White-Box Adversarial Examples for Text Classification”","“Universal Adversarial Triggers for Attacking and Analyzing NLP.”","code","“Robust Conversational Agents against Imperceptible Toxicity Triggers.”","“Universal and Transferable Adversarial Attacks on Aligned 